WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.617
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:09.640
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:15.785
Unit nineteen: Future of Work. Today you will predict change using will / is likely to.

4
00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:18.889
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.020
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:24.919
Automation.

7
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:30.122
Automation means the use of machines to perform tasks once done by people.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:33.691
Automation replaced many routine factory jobs.

9
00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:36.959
Your turn. Say: Automation.

10
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:40.743
Automation.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.705
Gig economy.

12
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:48.061
Gig economy means work arranged through short-term tasks and platforms.

13
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:52.356
The gig economy offers flexibility but little security.

14
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.591
Your turn. Say: Gig economy.

15
00:00:58.433 --> 00:00:59.434
Gig economy.

16
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:01.173
Reskilling.

17
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:05.472
Reskilling means training workers for new kinds of jobs.

18
00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:09.174
Reskilling helped drivers become logistics planners.

19
00:01:09.783 --> 00:01:12.621
Your turn. Say: Reskilling.

20
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:16.584
Reskilling.

21
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:18.409
Remote work.

22
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:23.100
Remote work means doing a job from outside a central workplace.

23
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.217
Remote work widened the pool of candidates.

24
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:29.666
Your turn. Say: Remote work.

25
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.826
Remote work.

26
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:35.782
Labor market.

27
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:40.292
Labor market means the system in which workers and employers meet.

28
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:43.498
The labor market rewards digital skills.

29
00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:46.736
Your turn. Say: Labor market.

30
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.798
Labor market.

31
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.399
Universal basic income.

32
00:01:54.383 --> 00:01:58.322
Universal basic income means a regular payment given to every citizen.

33
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:02.576
Universal basic income is being trialled in several countries.

34
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:07.043
Your turn. Say: Universal basic income.

35
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.772
Universal basic income.

36
00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:13.785
Job displacement.

37
00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:19.279
Job displacement means the loss of jobs when tasks are automated or moved.

38
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:22.649
Job displacement hit textile workers first.

39
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.891
Your turn. Say: Job displacement.

40
00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:30.338
Job displacement.

41
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.839
Workforce transformation.

42
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:38.787
Workforce transformation means a large-scale change in how people work.

43
00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:42.405
Workforce transformation will demand lifelong learning.

44
00:02:43.017 --> 00:02:46.226
Your turn. Say: Workforce transformation.

45
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.973
Workforce transformation.

46
00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:55.650
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.483 --> 00:03:00.131
Visal, will robots take everyone's job in ten years?

48
00:03:01.217 --> 00:03:07.979
Unlikely. Automation is likely to reshape many industries rather than abolish work altogether.

49
00:03:09.083 --> 00:03:11.141
But some jobs will disappear, right?

50
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:20.580
Certainly. Routine tasks are likely to be automated first, and job displacement will be painful without reskilling.

51
00:03:21.683 --> 00:03:23.648
What will new jobs look like?

52
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:36.075
Many will be hybrid roles combining human judgment with digital tools. Remote work is expected to remain common, and the gig economy will probably keep growing.

53
00:03:37.233 --> 00:03:38.664
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:43.571
Grammar focus: Future prediction language (will, is likely to).

55
00:03:43.871 --> 00:03:45.128
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.817 --> 00:03:48.675
Automation is likely to reshape many industries.

57
00:03:49.583 --> 00:03:52.721
Routine tasks will probably be automated first.

58
00:03:53.617 --> 00:03:56.069
Remote work is expected to remain common.

59
00:03:57.033 --> 00:04:06.652
Predict with will / be likely to / be expected to / be set to + base verb, softened by probably, possibly. 'Likely' is an adjective: is likely to (not 'is probably to').

60
00:04:07.417 --> 00:04:11.799
Be careful! Don't say: Automation is likely reshape industries.

61
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:17.416
Say: Automation is likely to reshape industries.

62
00:04:17.716 --> 00:04:19.600
likely + to-infinitive.

63
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.215
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.524
Work in the Next Decade.

65
00:04:25.824 --> 00:04:31.329
Forecasts about work are uncertain, but several trends are widely expected to continue.

66
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:41.148
Automation is likely to reshape many industries, especially those based on routine tasks, while creating demand for digital and creative skills.

67
00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:47.709
Job displacement will fall unevenly, so reskilling is expected to become a lifelong necessity.

68
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:56.179
Remote work and the gig economy will probably expand, offering flexibility while raising questions about security and benefits.

69
00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:02.521
Some governments are testing universal basic income as a safeguard during workforce transformation.

70
00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:07.461
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.317 --> 00:05:11.326
Automation is likely blank reshape many industries.

72
00:05:11.626 --> 00:05:14.804
A, to. B, for. C, that.

73
00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:20.122
The answer is A: to.

74
00:05:21.283 --> 00:05:24.096
Training workers for new kinds of jobs is blank.

75
00:05:24.396 --> 00:05:28.688
A, reskilling. B, automation. C, displacement.

76
00:05:32.233 --> 00:05:34.059
The answer is A: reskilling.

77
00:05:35.217 --> 00:05:38.366
A regular payment given to every citizen is blank.

78
00:05:38.666 --> 00:05:43.410
A, a gig. B, universal basic income. C, remote work.

79
00:05:46.967 --> 00:05:49.976
The answer is B: universal basic income.

80
00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:52.884
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:53.184 --> 00:06:00.077
A, Jobs will probably change.. B, Jobs probably will to change.. C, Jobs are probably to will change..

82
00:06:03.633 --> 00:06:07.014
The answer is A: Jobs will probably change..

83
00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.241
According to the reading, reskilling is expected to become…

84
00:06:11.541 --> 00:06:16.170
A, unnecessary. B, a lifelong necessity. C, illegal.

85
00:06:19.733 --> 00:06:22.464
The answer is B: a lifelong necessity.

86
00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:26.946
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.783 --> 00:06:30.805
How do you think the workplace will change over the next decade?

88
00:06:47.617 --> 00:06:49.082
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:49.382 --> 00:07:12.601
I think the workplace will become more flexible and more digital. Routine tasks are likely to be automated, so people will need skills such as critical thinking, communication and adaptability. Remote and hybrid work will probably remain common. Workers will have to keep learning throughout their careers, and employers will need to support them.

90
00:07:12.901 --> 00:07:14.529
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:15.417 --> 00:07:20.809
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future prediction language (will, is likely to).

92
00:07:21.109 --> 00:07:25.874
Today's value is future readiness. Build the skills that tomorrow will ask of you.

93
00:07:26.174 --> 00:07:28.734
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.034 --> 00:07:31.965
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

